From: ClauCoDillo NuttX port
Subject: [PATCH] mwin: bound the *real* MwSelect() mouse/keyboard drain loops

Corrects an incomplete earlier fix (0002-mwin-give-mouse-and-keyboard-
independent-event-budgets.patch): that patch bounded the mouse/
keyboard drain loops in the `#else /* MSDOS | _MINIX | NDS | VXWORKS |
PSP | __MINGW32__ | ALLEGRO | EMSCRIPTEN | AJAGUAR */` MwSelect()
variant. That variant is *not* the one this build actually compiles --
`#if UNIX && HAVE_SELECT` is true for this Linux sim build, so an
earlier, completely different MwSelect() (the real select()-based one)
is the active definition. 0002's fix was applied to dead code the
entire time.

The *real* MwSelect() (the `UNIX && HAVE_SELECT` one) had the exact
same unbounded-loop shape, just never caught in the same pass:

    if (mouse_fd >= 0 && FD_ISSET(mouse_fd, &rfds))
        while (MwCheckMouseEvent())
            continue;

No cap at all -- if mouse events keep arriving as fast as each
MwCheckMouseEvent() call can service them (sustained rapid physical
mouse movement, or a burst of X11 MotionNotify events), this loop
never returns. Confirmed as the actual root cause of a real,
repeatedly-reproduced bug: the whole browser window becomes
unresponsive (100% CPU, force-close required) during ordinary mouse
movement. Verified directly: `/proc/<pid>/stat` during a hang showed
enormous `utime` and ~0 `stime` (a pure userspace compute spin, not
blocked on I/O), and CPU time climbed steadily rather than settling --
a genuine infinite loop, not a large-but-finite backlog draining.
Keyboard servicing sits immediately after this loop in the same
function and was never reached while it spun, which also explains why
keyboard input was itself unreliable under similar conditions.

Fix: cap both drain loops at 10 events per MwSelect() call, same
policy 0002 already established for the (inactive, for this build)
other MwSelect() variant. Verified with extensive stress-testing
(dozens of rounds of rapid synthetic mouse movement, far exceeding
what previously triggered a hang within 3-5 rounds) showing zero
hangs and CPU settling back to idle between bursts.

--- a/src/mwin/winmain.c
+++ b/src/mwin/winmain.c
@@ -3,6 +3,7 @@
  *
  * Main module of Microwindows
  */
+#include <stdio.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
 #include <string.h>
 #include <signal.h>
@@ -321,15 +322,31 @@
 	/* Wait for some input on any of the fds in the set or a timeout*/
 	if ((e = select(setsize, &rfds, &wfds, &efds, to)) > 0)
 	{
-		/* service mouse file descriptor*/
+		int mouseevents = 0;
+		int kbdevents = 0;
+
+		/* service mouse file descriptor. Bounded (unlike the original
+		 * unconditional "while (...) continue;"): if events keep
+		 * arriving as fast as they're drained (e.g. sustained rapid
+		 * mouse motion, X11 MotionNotify faster than each
+		 * MwCheckMouseEvent() call can service), this loop never
+		 * returned at all -- a genuine, reproducible 100% CPU hang,
+		 * with keyboard servicing (right below, never reached while
+		 * this spins) starved for as long as it ran. Capped the same
+		 * way the *other* (platform-conditional, not actually
+		 * compiled for this Unix/select() build) MwSelect() already
+		 * caps its own two drain loops -- this one just never got the
+		 * same treatment. */
 		if (mouse_fd >= 0 && FD_ISSET(mouse_fd, &rfds))
 			while (MwCheckMouseEvent())
-				continue;
+				if (++mouseevents > 10)
+					break;
 
 		/* service keyboard file descriptor*/
 		if (keyb_fd >= 0 && FD_ISSET(keyb_fd, &rfds))
 			while (MwCheckKeyboardEvent())
-				continue;
+				if (++kbdevents > 10)
+					break;
 
 		/* If registered descriptor, handle it */
 		fd = userregfd_head;
